 "Cars and travel":["Why did the car get a promotion? It drove the business forward.", "What do you call a sleeping bull driving a car? A bulldozer at the wheel.", "Why don’t cars ever get lonely? They come with a lot of passengers.", "What kind of car does a snake drive? An Ana-Honda.", "Why did the traffic light turn red? You would too if people stared at you while you changed.", "What did the suitcase say on holiday? I’m a little packed.", "Why was the road so tired? It had been run over all day.", "How do trees get online? They log in from the road trip.", "What has four wheels and flies? A rubbish truck.", "Why did the map go to school? To get a little direction."],
 "Maths":["Why was six afraid of seven? Because seven eight nine.", "What do you call friends who love maths? Algebros.", "Why should you never argue with a 90-degree angle? It’s always right.", "Parallel lines have so much in common. It’s a shame they’ll never meet.", "What did the zero say to the eight? Nice belt.", "Why is the obtuse angle always upset? It’s never right.", "How do you stay warm in a cold room? Go to the corner. It’s 90 degrees.", "I’ll do algebra, I’ll do trig, I’ll even do statistics. But graphing is where I draw the line.", "Why did the student wear glasses in maths class? To improve di-vision.", "What’s a maths teacher’s favourite beat? Four on the floor, divisible by two."],
 "History":["Why were the early days of history called the Dark Ages? Too many knights.", "How did the Vikings send secret messages? By Norse code.", "What did they do at the Boston Tea Party? I don’t know, I wasn’t invited.", "Why did the pharaoh boast? He was full of sphinx.", "Where did the kings keep their armies? Up their sleevies.", "What was the most popular dance in ancient Rome? The Caesar shuffle.", "Why did the mummy go on holiday? He needed to unwind.", "Why are history teachers so calm? They know nothing lasts forever.", "What did Mason say to Dixon? We’ve got to draw the line somewhere.", "How do you find a lost Roman? Ask for directions in Latin, then follow the signs."],
